\begin{equation}
\tau(e) = \max(\tau_{init} \cdot \exp(-\lambda \cdot p), \tau_{final})
\end{equation}

where $\tau(e)$ is the threshold at epoch $e$, $p = e/E_{total}$ represents progress, $\tau_{init} = 0.9$ and $\tau_{final} = 0.3$ define initial and final thresholds, and $\lambda = 3$ controls decay rate. This strategy ensures stable early learning on high-quality data while gradually exposing the model to degraded conditions representative of operational scenarios.

\begin{figure}[!h]
      \centering

  \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/quality_aware_training.png}
      \caption{Quality-aware training framework integrating multi-source  metadata (GFSC QC, Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT/WVP) through multiplicative loss  weighting. Curriculum learning progressively transitions from high-confidence samples ($\tau=0.9$) to challenging degraded conditions  ($\tau=0.3$) via exponential decay. The uncertainty quantification head  provides well-calibrated total uncertainty estimates (ECE=0.025) with physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$).}
      \label{fig:quality_aware_training}
  \end{figure}

\textbf{Uncertainty Quantification.} The framework provides pixel-wise uncertainty estimates through a dedicated prediction head that outputs total uncertainty reflecting both data quality and model confidence. Unlike approaches that decompose aleatoric and epistemic uncertainties through specialized architectures or Monte Carlo sampling, our implementation focuses on total uncertainty for operational efficiency while maintaining excellent calibration properties (ECE=0.025). The uncertainty head employs softplus activation to ensure positive values: $\sigma^2 = \log(1 + \exp(\mathbf{z}))$ where $\mathbf{z}$ is the raw network output. Uncertainty estimates are regularized through negative log-likelihood loss encouraging higher uncertainty for larger prediction errors:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{uncertainty} = -\frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma^2) - \frac{(\mathbf{y} - \hat{\mathbf{y}})^2}{2\sigma^2}
\end{equation}

