\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:proposed_method}

\subsection{Overview}

Our methodology addresses the challenge of high-resolution fractional snow cover estimation through a hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture that fuses Sentinel-1 SAR (10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 bands unified at 10m). The framework integrates three principal innovations: multi-resolution processing that enables training at 60m resolution while producing 10m predictions, cross-attention fusion that explicitly models inter-sensor dependencies, and quality-aware learning that leverages auxiliary metadata for robust training under heterogeneous data conditions. Figure~\ref{fig:overall_methodology} illustrates the complete processing pipeline from satellite data acquisition through preprocessing, multi-modal fusion, and high-resolution prediction.  

\added{\textbf{Distinction from Prior Work.} While SAR-optical fusion and multi-resolution training have been explored independently in remote sensing literature, existing approaches face specific limitations that our methodology addresses \cite{clerici2017fusion}, \cite{scarpa2018cnn}, \cite{ebel2021fusing} (fusion), \cite{li2022deep}, \cite{meraner2020cloud} (multi-resolution). First, conventional fusion strategies (concatenation, late fusion, attention-weighted averaging) treat modalities symmetrically without explicitly modeling \textit{how} information from one sensor should inform interpretation of the other \cite{hazirbas2016fusenet} (concatenation), \cite{orynbaikyzy2020crop} (feature stacking + decision fusion); our bidirectional cross-attention enables SAR features to selectively query relevant optical context and vice versa through learned query-key-value projections (Section~\ref{sec:cross_attention}). Second, multi-resolution approaches typically employ fixed aggregation (average/max pooling) that ignores scene content \cite{li2022deep}; our adaptive aggregation learns to preserve snow-relevant information through content-aware pooling decisions (Section~\ref{sec:multi_resolution}). Third, existing quality-aware methods often use binary masks or single-source quality indicators \cite{lin2024robust}, \cite{gascoin2019theia}; our framework integrates heterogeneous metadata from multiple sources (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) through multiplicative weighting with curriculum scheduling (Section~\ref{sec:quality_aware}). The novelty lies not in the individual concepts but in their principled integration within a unified architecture designed for the specific challenges of operational satellite-based snow monitoring.}

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/overall_methodology.png}
    \caption{The model consists of dual-path CNN backbones with modality-specific attention (CBAM for Sentinel-1 SAR, ECA for Sentinel-2 optical) that extract multi-scale   features through four stages (64, 128, 256, 512 channels). Shallow Feature Fusion (SFF) modules merge  cross-modal information at each scale, with skip connections (orange dashed arrows) propagating  multi-resolution features to the decoder. Three novel contributions are highlighted: (1) Multi-Resolution  Processing enables training on coarse 60m labels while processing fine 10-20m inputs through learned  adaptive aggregation and guided disaggregation, (2) the Fusion Vision Transformer (FVit) employs learnable  adaptive fusion weights ($\lambda_{sa}$, $\lambda_{ca}$) in its Ada-MBA stage to dynamically balance  self-attention and cross-attention based on data quality, and (3) Quality-Aware Training incorporates cloud   masks and quality flags through confidence-weighted loss with curriculum learning. The decoder produces  dual outputs: fractional snow cover prediction $\hat{y}$ and uncertainty estimate $\sigma^2$.}
    \label{fig:overall_methodology}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Data Preparation.} Sentinel-1 Ground Range Detected (GRD) products undergo standard SAR preprocessing including radiometric calibration to sigma-nought backscatter, Enhanced Lee Sigma speckle filtering (7$\times$7 window), and Range-Doppler orthorectification using SRTM 30m DEM \cite{kaushik2018comparative}. Sentinel-2 Level-1C products are atmospherically corrected using a hybrid Dark Object Subtraction and MODTRAN-based approach, incorporating Rayleigh scattering, aerosol transmission (AOT at 550nm), and water vapor absorption corrections \cite{sola2018assessment}. Both modalities are geometrically co-registered through bilinear resampling to a common 10m grid aligned with UTM coordinates. Temporal pairing selects Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 acquisitions within 3 days using Gaussian temporal weighting ($\sigma_t = 1.5$ days) to minimize snow condition changes between observations.

Snow-specific spectral indices are computed from Sentinel-2 bands to provide auxiliary features: the Normalized Difference Snow Index NDSI $= (\rho_{B3} - \rho_{B11})/(\rho_{B3} + \rho_{B11})$ exploits snow's high visible and low SWIR reflectance, Enhanced NDSI incorporates NDVI correction to reduce vegetation interference, and the Snow Cover Area Index combines NIR-SWIR differences with vegetation masking \cite{voitik2023comparison}. Quality metadata from Sentinel-2 Scene Classification Layer (SCL), cloud probability masks, and GFSC quality control layers (QC, QCFLAGS) provide spatially explicit data quality indicators used during training and inference.

\subsection{Multi-Resolution Processing Framework}
\label{sec:multi_resolution}

The multi-resolution framework addresses a fundamental challenge in operational satellite monitoring: high-resolution sensors (Sentinel-1/2 at 10m) lack corresponding high-resolution reference data, with widely-available products like GFSC providing only 60m resolution. Traditional approaches either train at coarse resolution (sacrificing input detail) or directly upscale labels (introducing interpolation artifacts). Our approach operates at three resolution stages: high-resolution input fusion at 10m, adaptive aggregation to 60m for supervised training, and guided disaggregation back to 10m for enhanced prediction (Figure~\ref{fig:multi_resolution_schematic}).

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/Multiresoulution_processing_framwork.png}
    \caption{Multi-resolution processing framework. The novel training strategy fuses Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data at 10m resolution, aggregates features to 60m for supervised training against GFSC reference data with quality-aware loss weighting, and disaggregates predictions back to 10m through guided upsampling that preserves fine-scale spatial detail.}
    \label{fig:multi_resolution_schematic}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Resolution Aggregation ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m}$).} During training, high-resolution features are aggregated to match the 60m resolution of GFSC reference data through adaptive pooling that preserves snow-relevant information. \added{Unlike fixed pooling operations (average or max) that apply uniformly regardless of scene content, our aggregation adapts based on learned snow probability---using max pooling in snow-dominated regions to preserve peak signals and average pooling elsewhere to maintain spatial consistency. This content-aware design prevents the information loss that occurs when fixed aggregation averages out fine-scale snow boundaries.} The aggregation function employs snow-aware weighting:

\begin{equation}
A_{adaptive}(X_{10m}) =
\begin{cases}
\max_{(i,j) \in W_{6\times6}} X_{10m}(i,j) & \text{if } P_{snow}(W_{6\times6}) > \tau_{snow} \\
\frac{1}{36} \sum_{(i,j) \in W_{6\times6}} X_{10m}(i,j) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

where $W_{6\times6}$ represents the 6$\times$6 aggregation window corresponding to the 6:1 resolution ratio, and $P_{snow}$ is the learned snow probability within the window. This adaptive strategy employs max pooling for snow-dominated regions (preserving peak snow signals) and average pooling elsewhere (maintaining spatial consistency). The snow probability is computed through a lightweight detection network: $P_{snow}(W) = \frac{1}{|W|} \sum_{(i,j) \in W} \sigma(f_{snow}(X_{10m}(i,j)))$, where $\sigma$ is sigmoid activation and $f_{snow}$ is a learned detection function with threshold $\tau_{snow} = 0.7$.

\textbf{Guided Disaggregation ($60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$).} During inference, coarse-resolution predictions are disaggregated to 10m through guided filtering that leverages high-resolution input features to recover fine-scale spatial detail. The disaggregation employs a modified guided filter framework \cite{he2012guided}:

\begin{equation}
\hat{X}_{10m} = a \cdot G_{10m} + b
\end{equation}

where $G_{10m}$ is the high-resolution guidance image derived from input Sentinel-1/2 features, and coefficients $a$ and $b$ are computed through local linear regression:

\begin{equation}
a = \frac{\text{Cov}(G, X)}{\text{Var}(G) + \epsilon}, \quad b = \mu_X - a \cdot \mu_G
\end{equation}

Here $\mu_G$ and $\mu_X$ are local means of guidance and upsampled data, computed over sliding windows. The regularization parameter $\epsilon = 10^{-6}$ prevents division by zero in homogeneous regions. This guided approach preserves edge information from high-resolution inputs while maintaining consistency with coarse-resolution predictions, enabling the model to generate enhanced 10m outputs that capture sub-60m spatial patterns absent in training targets.

\textbf{Multi-Scale Loss Function.} Training incorporates multi-scale supervision to ensure consistency across resolution levels:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{multi\text{-}scale} = \sum_{k=0}^{3} \lambda_k \cdot \mathcal{L}_{MSE}(\hat{Y}_k, Y_k)
\end{equation}

where $\lambda_k = 2^{-k}$ provides higher weight to finer scales, $\hat{Y}_k$ and $Y_k$ are predictions and targets at scale $k$, with $k=0$ corresponding to 10m resolution. This multi-scale formulation encourages the model to maintain spatial coherence across the resolution hierarchy while focusing primary supervision at the target 60m scale.

\subsection{Hybrid CNN-Transformer Architecture}
\label{sec:cross_attention}

The hybrid architecture synergistically combines convolutional neural networks for local feature extraction with vision transformers for global context modeling, specifically designed for multi-modal satellite data fusion. The architecture comprises four interconnected components: dual-path CNN backbones for modality-specific processing, cross-attention fusion for intelligent multi-modal integration, spatial transformers for landscape-scale dependency modeling, and feature pyramid networks for multi-scale representation.

\textbf{Dual-Path CNN Backbones.} Separate CNN pathways process Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data through structurally similar but parameter-independent networks, acknowledging fundamental differences between SAR backscatter and optical reflectance. Each pathway follows a ResNet-inspired architecture with four progressive stages featuring channel dimensions [64, 128, 256, 512] and spatial downsampling factors [4, 8, 16, 32]. Convolutional blocks incorporate group normalization (32 groups) to handle varying batch sizes, GELU activation for smooth gradients preserving subtle snow boundaries, and modality-specific attention mechanisms. Sentinel-1 features employ Convolutional Block Attention Modules (CBAM) to enhance both channel and spatial attention for SAR texture patterns, while Sentinel-2 features utilize Efficient Channel Attention (ECA) to emphasize spectral relationships critical for optical snow detection \cite{woo2018cbam, wang2020eca}.

\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/cnn_backbone_arch.png}
    \caption{Dual-path CNN backbone architecture showing separate processing pathways for Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data, with modality-specific attention mechanisms and progressive feature extraction through four hierarchical stages.}
    \label{fig:cnn_backbone}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Cross-Attention Fusion Mechanism.} Multi-modal fusion employs bidirectional cross-attention to explicitly model inter-sensor dependencies. \added{Unlike early fusion (concatenation) that treats modalities as additional channels, or late fusion (ensemble) that combines independent predictions, cross-attention enables \textit{selective information exchange}: each modality learns to query task-relevant features from the other. This is particularly important for SAR-optical fusion where the relationship is asymmetric---SAR provides structural/textural information useful when optical data is cloud-contaminated, while optical spectral signatures disambiguate SAR backscatter variations caused by moisture, roughness, or snow wetness.}  

For features $\mathbf{F}_{S1} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C_1 \times H \times W}$ from Sentinel-1 and $\mathbf{F}_{S2} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times C_2 \times H \times W}$ from Sentinel-2, the mechanism computes query-key-value attention in both directions:

\begin{equation}
\text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V}) = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{Q}\mathbf{K}^T}{\sqrt{d_k}}\right)\mathbf{V}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{F}_{S1 \rightarrow S2} = \text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}_{S1}, \mathbf{K}_{S2}, \mathbf{V}_{S2}), \quad \mathbf{F}_{S2 \rightarrow S1} = \text{Attention}(\mathbf{Q}_{S2}, \mathbf{K}_{S1}, \mathbf{V}_{S1})
\end{equation}

where queries, keys, and values are derived through learned linear projections. The fused representation combines both directional flows: $\mathbf{F}_{fused} = \text{LayerNorm}(\text{Linear}([\mathbf{F}_{S1 \rightarrow S2}; \mathbf{F}_{S2 \rightarrow S1}]))$. The cross-attention employs 8 attention heads with dimension $d_k = 64$, enabling the model to capture different aspects of cross-modal relationships simultaneously, texture correlations, intensity relationships, and spatial pattern correspondences between SAR and optical signatures.

\begin{figure}[!h]
      \centering

  \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/cross_attention_fusion.png}
      \caption{Cross-attention fusion mechanism showing bidirectional query-key-value attention between Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 features. The mechanism computes S1→S2 and S2→S1 attention maps using 8 parallel heads with dimension $d_k=64$, enabling each modality to query relevant information from the other for intelligent multi-modal integration.}
      \label{fig:cross_attention_fusion}
  \end{figure}

\textbf{Spatial Transformer.} The transformer component addresses convolutional limitations in capturing long-range spatial dependencies essential for understanding landscape-scale snow distribution patterns influenced by topography and regional climate. The spatial self-attention mechanism preserves 2D topology through learnable relative position encoding:

\begin{equation}
\text{SpatialAttention}(\mathbf{Q}, \mathbf{K}, \mathbf{V})_{i,j} = \text{softmax}\left(\frac{\mathbf{q}_i \mathbf{k}_j^T}{\sqrt{d_k}} + \mathbf{B}_{i,j}\right)\mathbf{v}_j
\end{equation}

where $\mathbf{B}_{i,j} = \mathbf{W}_B[\text{rel\_pos}(i, j)]$ represents learnable relative position bias capturing spatial relationships between pixels. This formulation enables the model to learn that nearby pixels have stronger influence while allowing long-range interactions when topographically or climatically relevant. The transformer architecture employs 6 sequential blocks with 8 attention heads and 512 embedding dimensions, providing sufficient capacity for complex spatial relationships while operating on compact feature representations (H/32$\times$W/32) to maintain computational efficiency.

\textbf{Feature Pyramid Network.} Multi-scale processing through FPN addresses snow cover's inherent scale variability, from pixel-level presence to landscape-level distribution trends. The FPN incorporates adaptive scale selection through attention-weighted fusion:

\begin{equation}
\mathbf{F}_{pyramid}^{(l)} = \mathbf{A}^{(l)} \odot \mathbf{L}^{(l)} + (1 - \mathbf{A}^{(l)}) \odot \text{Upsample}(\mathbf{F}_{pyramid}^{(l+1)})
\end{equation}

where $\mathbf{A}^{(l)} = \sigma(\text{Conv}_{1 \times 1}(\text{GAP}(\mathbf{L}^{(l)})))$ represents learned attention weights determining optimal spatial scale for different scenarios. The FPN processes features at four pyramid levels (P2, P3, P4, P5) with 256-channel feature maps, progressively incorporating finer-scale information through bilinear upsampling and attention-weighted fusion from coarsest (P5) to finest (P2) levels.

\subsection{Quality-Aware Learning Framework}
\label{sec:quality_aware}

The quality-aware framework addresses challenges of training on heterogeneous satellite data with spatially and temporally varying quality through adaptive loss weighting, curriculum learning, and uncertainty quantification.

\added{\textbf{Distinction from Standard Training.} Conventional deep learning treats all training samples equally, assuming homogeneous data quality. This assumption is systematically violated in satellite imagery where cloud contamination, atmospheric interference, sensor artifacts, and reference data uncertainty vary spatially and temporally. Simple binary masking (excluding low-quality pixels) discards potentially useful information, while equal weighting allows noisy samples to corrupt learned representations. Our approach instead modulates the contribution of each pixel through continuous quality weights derived from multiple independent metadata sources, combined with curriculum scheduling that progressively introduces challenging samples as the model develops robustness.}

\textbf{Confidence-Weighted Training.} Quality information from multiple sources---Sentinel-2 SCL flags, cloud probability masks, GFSC QC layers, terrain complexity indices---is integrated through multiplicative loss weighting:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{quality} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} w_i^{conf} \cdot w_i^{qual} \cdot \mathcal{L}_{base}(y_i, \hat{y}_i)
\end{equation}

where $w_i^{conf} = \sigma(c_i)^\alpha$ represents confidence weight from model predictions with strength parameter $\alpha$, and $w_i^{qual} = \prod_{j=1}^{K} q_{i,j}^{\beta_j}$ denotes quality mask weight from auxiliary indicators. This combined weighting emphasizes high-quality samples while maintaining gradient flow for challenging cases, enabling the model to learn robust representations without requiring extensive manual data curation.

\textbf{Curriculum Learning.} Training progresses through a curriculum that transitions from high-confidence to challenging samples via exponential decay of confidence thresholds:

\begin{equation}
\tau(e) = \max(\tau_{init} \cdot \exp(-\lambda \cdot p), \tau_{final})
\end{equation}

where $\tau(e)$ is the threshold at epoch $e$, $p = e/E_{total}$ represents progress, $\tau_{init} = 0.9$ and $\tau_{final} = 0.3$ define initial and final thresholds, and $\lambda = 3$ controls decay rate. This strategy ensures stable early learning on high-quality data while gradually exposing the model to degraded conditions representative of operational scenarios.

\begin{figure}[!h]
      \centering

  \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images/quality_aware_training.png}
      \caption{Quality-aware training framework integrating multi-source  metadata (GFSC QC, Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT/WVP) through multiplicative loss  weighting. Curriculum learning progressively transitions from high-confidence samples ($\tau=0.9$) to challenging degraded conditions  ($\tau=0.3$) via exponential decay. The uncertainty quantification head  provides well-calibrated total uncertainty estimates (ECE=0.025) with physically realistic spatial patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$).}
      \label{fig:quality_aware_training}
  \end{figure}

\textbf{Uncertainty Quantification.} The framework provides pixel-wise uncertainty estimates through a dedicated prediction head that outputs total uncertainty reflecting both data quality and model confidence. Unlike approaches that decompose aleatoric and epistemic uncertainties through specialized architectures or Monte Carlo sampling, our implementation focuses on total uncertainty for operational efficiency while maintaining excellent calibration properties (ECE=0.025). The uncertainty head employs softplus activation to ensure positive values: $\sigma^2 = \log(1 + \exp(\mathbf{z}))$ where $\mathbf{z}$ is the raw network output. Uncertainty estimates are regularized through negative log-likelihood loss encouraging higher uncertainty for larger prediction errors:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{uncertainty} = -\frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi\sigma^2) - \frac{(\mathbf{y} - \hat{\mathbf{y}})^2}{2\sigma^2}
\end{equation}

This formulation provides well-calibrated confidence estimates that enable targeted quality control and risk-informed decision-making in downstream applications.

\textbf{Complete Loss Function.} The total training objective integrates multiple components addressing prediction accuracy, spatial consistency, and uncertainty calibration:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{total} = \alpha \mathcal{L}_{regression} + \beta \mathcal{L}_{perceptual} + \gamma \mathcal{L}_{consistency} + \delta \mathcal{L}_{uncertainty}
\end{equation}

The primary regression loss employs smooth L1 formulation with quality weighting: $\mathcal{L}_{regression} = \frac{1}{|\Omega|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \Omega} w_{i,j} \cdot \text{SmoothL1}(y_{i,j}, \hat{y}_{i,j})$ where $\Omega$ represents valid pixels. 

Perceptual consistency enforces multi-scale feature-level similarity through VGG-based perceptual loss \cite{johnson2016perceptual}: $\mathcal{L}_{perceptual} = \sum_{l=1}^{L} \lambda_l \|\Phi_l(\mathbf{y}) - \Phi_l(\hat{\mathbf{y}})\|_2^2$. 

Spatial consistency maintains local coherence via gradient matching: $\mathcal{L}_{consistency} = \|\nabla \mathbf{y} - \nabla \hat{\mathbf{y}}\|_1$. 

Loss component weights $(\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta)$ are learned through gradient-based optimization rather than manual tuning, enabling adaptive balancing throughout training.

\subsection{Training and Implementation}

\textbf{Training Protocol.} The model is trained end-to-end using AdamW optimizer \cite{loshchilov2017decoupled} with weight decay $1 \times 10^{-4}$ and effective batch size 64 achieved through gradient accumulation. Learning rate follows cosine annealing with warm restarts, initializing at $1 \times 10^{-3}$ and decaying to $1 \times 10^{-5}$ over 100 epochs. Mixed-precision training (FP16) with dynamic loss scaling reduces memory requirements by approximately 40\% while maintaining numerical stability. Regularization includes dropout (0.1), stochastic depth (0.1), and label smoothing (0.1) to improve generalization. Training employs random cropping to 100$\times$100 patches, horizontal and vertical flipping, random rotation ($\pm$15$^\circ$), and Gaussian noise augmentation ($\sigma = 0.01$) to enhance robustness.

\textbf{Computational Efficiency.} The architecture balances accuracy with computational feasibility through strategic design choices. Grouped normalization reduces parameters compared to instance normalization, hierarchical CNN structure minimizes complexity via progressive downsampling, and transformer operation on compact representations (H/32$\times$W/32) significantly reduces quadratic self-attention complexity. Gradient checkpointing for transformer layers trades computation for memory savings. The complete architecture processes 100$\times$100 patches with approximately 45M parameters and 12 GFLOPs, achieving inference speeds suitable for operational applications while enabling training on standard GPU hardware (32GB memory).

\textbf{Inference Strategy.} For images exceeding 100$\times$100 pixels, inference employs sliding window prediction with 64-pixel overlap. Overlapping regions are blended using Gaussian weights to eliminate boundary artifacts, ensuring spatially smooth predictions across patch boundaries. Test-time augmentation with 8-fold geometric transformations (rotations and reflections) further enhances prediction reliability, with ensemble averaging improving accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%.

