   218→\section*{Response to Reviewer 2}
   219→
   220→\textbf{R2-1: Evaluation protocol for 10m predictions against 60m ground truth}
   221→\begin{quote}
   222→\textit{``Since the ground truth (GFSC) is 60 m, how are the 10 m predictions evaluated quantitatively? Did the authors downsample their 10m predictions back to 60 m to calculate MAE against the GFSC labels? Or did they upsample the GFSC labels to 10m? [...] The authors should ideally provide a small subset of `true' high-resolution ground truth to demonstrate that the model actually recovers 10 m details and not just artifacts.''}
   223→\end{quote}
   224→
   225→We thank the reviewer for raising this important methodological question, which deserves explicit clarification. We have added a new paragraph titled ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' in Section 4.1 to address this concern directly.
   226→
   227→\textbf{Answer to the specific questions:}
   228→
   229→\begin{enumerate}
   230→    \item \textbf{How are predictions evaluated?} The model is trained and evaluated entirely at \textbf{60m resolution}. We do \textit{not} downsample 10m predictions to 60m, nor do we upsample GFSC labels to 10m.
   231→
   232→    \item \textbf{The actual workflow:}
   233→    \begin{itemize}
   234→        \item Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are \textbf{aggregated to 60m} through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks before being fed to the model
   235→        \item The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution
   236→        \item All metrics (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, $R^2$, etc.) are computed at 60m against GFSC labels
   237→        \item This is implemented in our codebase: \texttt{aggregate\_10m\_to\_60m()} function applies mean aggregation over $6 \times 6$ blocks (e.g., 100$\times$100 pixels at 10m $\rightarrow$ 16$\times$16 pixels at 60m)
   238→    \end{itemize}
   239→
   240→    \item \textbf{What about the ``10m prediction'' claim?} The multi-resolution framework ($10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) enables optional \textit{inference-time} disaggregation from 60m predictions back to 10m through guided upsampling that leverages high-resolution input features. This is a capability for operational deployment, not part of the training/validation pipeline.
   241→\end{enumerate}
   242→
   243→\textbf{Clarification added to manuscript:}
   244→
   245→We have added the following explicit statement in Section 4.1 (Experimental Setup):
   246→
   247→\begin{quote}
   248→``During training, Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are aggregated to 60m through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks to match the GFSC reference data resolution. The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution, and \textbf{all quantitative metrics reported in this paper are computed at 60m resolution} against GFSC labels. [...] However, we emphasize that the 10m outputs represent \textit{spatially enhanced predictions} guided by fine-scale input structure, not independently validated high-resolution products.''
   249→\end{quote}
   250→
   251→\textbf{Regarding high-resolution ground truth:}
   252→
   253→The reviewer correctly notes that ideally we would validate 10m predictions against true high-resolution ground truth. We acknowledge this limitation explicitly:
   254→
   255→\begin{itemize}
   256→    \item High-resolution ($<$60m) fractional snow cover ground truth is not widely available for our study region
   257→    \item The 10m predictions are validated \textit{qualitatively} by visual alignment with Sentinel-2 optical imagery boundaries (Figure 8 in the manuscript shows boundary preservation)
   258→    \item \textit{Quantitative} validation of sub-60m accuracy would require independent high-resolution ground truth from UAV surveys, airborne sensors, or Landsat-derived 30m products
   259→    \item We have added this as an explicit limitation and future work direction in Section 5.5
   260→\end{itemize}
   261→
   262→We believe this transparent clarification addresses the reviewer's concern. The methodology is scientifically sound: training at the resolution of available ground truth is standard practice in remote sensing, and the guided disaggregation capability provides operational value even without high-resolution validation.
   263→
   264→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 4.1 (new ``Evaluation Resolution and Protocol'' paragraph); Section 5.5 Limitation \#4 (expanded to clarify evaluation resolution).
   265→
   266→\textbf{R2-2: Geographic generalization concerns (alpine terrain)}
   267→\begin{quote}
   268→\textit{``The study focuses on 29 tiles in Northern Finland. [...] How does XFuse handle complex alpine terrain (Alps or Himalayas) where SAR geometric distortions are severe? The current dataset might not sufficiently challenge the cross-attention fusion mechanism's ability to handle missing SAR information due to shadows.''}
   269→\end{quote}
   270→We thank the reviewer for raising this critical question, which addresses perhaps the most significant limitation of our current evaluation. The reviewer is entirely correct: northern Finland's boreal landscape represents a favorable scenario for SAR-based fusion, and we agree that geographic transferability is the primary open question for our methodology. We have added a substantial new ``Geographic Transferability'' paragraph as Limitation \#2 in Section~5.5 that addresses each of the reviewer's specific concerns in detail.
   271→
   272→\textbf{(1) SAR Geometric Distortions in Alpine Terrain:}
   273→
   274→Northern Finland's gentle topography (typical slopes $<$5\textdegree) effectively minimizes the geometric distortions that plague SAR observations in mountainous regions. In alpine terrain, the interaction between Sentinel-1's nominal incidence angles (29\textdegree--46\textdegree) and steep slopes produces three distinct degradation mechanisms:
   275→\begin{itemize}
   276→    \item \textit{Foreshortening:} On foreslopes exceeding $\sim$20\textdegree, topographic relief is compressed in the range direction, degrading spatial resolution and mixing returns from different elevations.
   277→    \item \textit{Layover:} When slope angle approaches the complement of the incidence angle, returns from multiple ground positions collapse into the same range cell, producing physically uninterpretable pixels.
   278→    \item \textit{Radar shadow:} Steep back-slopes receive no radar illumination, creating complete signal voids.
   279→\end{itemize}
   280→These effects concentrate in precisely the high-altitude accumulation zones where snow monitoring is most critical for water resource management. Our results should therefore be interpreted as achievable under near-optimal SAR acquisition geometry.
   281→
   282→\textbf{(2) Snow Metamorphism and Regime-Dependent SAR Signatures:}
   283→
   284→The reviewer correctly identifies that SAR-snow interactions are highly dependent on snow type. Our study area's predominantly dry continental snow exhibits relatively stable C-band backscatter. In contrast:
   285→\begin{itemize}
   286→    \item \textit{Maritime snowpacks} (warm, dense, frequent melt--freeze) produce strong C-band absorption through liquid water content, causing dramatic backscatter changes during diurnal cycles.
   287→    \item \textit{Continental interior snowpacks} (cold, dry, faceted grains) exhibit volume scattering from depth hoar layers.
   288→    \item \textit{Transitional regimes} (e.g., Himalayan basins) display complex elevation-dependent diurnal stratification.
   289→\end{itemize}
   290→Feature representations learned from boreal Finland may not generalize to these different physical regimes without recalibration or domain adaptation.
   291→
   292→\textbf{(3) How the Architecture Could Address These Challenges:}
   293→
   294→While we have not validated XFuse in alpine terrain, two architectural design choices are directly relevant to the reviewer's concern about ``handling missing SAR information'':
   295→\begin{itemize}
   296→    \item The \textit{bidirectional cross-attention mechanism} enables each modality to selectively attend to the complementary modality through learned query-key-value projections. In SAR-degraded regions (shadow, layover), the cross-attention weights could theoretically shift toward optical spectral features-analogous to the mechanism's designed behavior under cloud contamination, but operating in the reverse modality direction. The asymmetric nature of cross-attention (each modality independently queries the other) is specifically designed for scenarios where one sensor provides degraded information.
   297→    \item The \textit{quality-aware training framework} already learns continuous sample weighting from heterogeneous metadata sources (SCL, AOT, WVP, QC flags). This framework provides a natural extension point: terrain-derived quality indicators-local incidence angle, DEM-based layover/shadow masks-could be incorporated as additional metadata channels, enabling the model to systematically down-weight geometrically distorted SAR observations during training.
   298→\end{itemize}
   299→However, we are careful to present these as \textit{architectural affordances} rather than demonstrated capabilities. Without validation on mountainous datasets, we cannot claim that the cross-attention mechanism will successfully adapt to SAR shadow regions in practice.
   300→
   301→\textbf{(4) Concrete Path Forward:}
   302→
   303→We identify four requirements for robust geographic generalization in the revised manuscript:
   304→\begin{enumerate}
   305→    \item Radiometric terrain correction of Sentinel-1 data using high-resolution DEMs
   306→    \item Incorporation of topographic variables (slope, aspect, curvature, sky view factor) as auxiliary inputs
   307→    \item Training on geographically diverse datasets spanning boreal, alpine, and maritime snow regimes
   308→    \item Domain adaptation techniques for efficient transfer to undersampled snow climate zones
   309→\end{enumerate}
   310→We frame multi-region validation as the primary direction for future work and state explicitly that our current results represent performance under favorable SAR conditions.
   311→
   312→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section~5.5 (new Limitation \#2: ``Geographic Transferability''-comprehensive discussion of SAR geometric distortions in alpine terrain, snow metamorphism regime effects, architectural mitigation properties, and concrete future work requirements).
   313→
   314→\textbf{R2-3: Baseline comparison fairness (multi-resolution head)}
   315→\begin{quote}
   316→\textit{``Are these baselines also equipped with the Multi-Resolution Processing (10m$\to$60m$\to$10m) head? If XFuse has the advantage of the guided upsampling head while baselines simply interpolate 60m predictions, the comparison is unfair.''}
   317→\end{quote}
   318→We appreciate this important question regarding experimental fairness. We confirm that \textbf{all baselines are trained and evaluated under identical conditions}, ensuring a fair comparison:
   319→
   320→\begin{itemize}
   321→    \item \textbf{Identical data pipeline:} All models-Early Fusion, CNN-Only, Transformer-Only, and XFuse-use the same dataset class (\texttt{EnhancedDualResolutionDataset} with \\ \texttt{train\_mode=True}), which aggregates Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs from native 10m resolution to 60m via mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks. No model receives higher-resolution inputs than any other.
   322→    \item \textbf{Identical training targets:} All models predict against the same 60m GFSC reference labels. All quantitative metrics (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, F1, $R^2$) are computed at 60m resolution.
   323→    \item \textbf{Multi-resolution head is inference-only:} The guided disaggregation ($60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$) described in Section~3.2 is a post-processing step applied \textit{after} the core prediction during inference. It is \textbf{not} used during training or quantitative evaluation, and therefore does not contribute to any reported metric.
   324→    \item \textbf{Ablation evidence confirms architectural gains:} The ablation study (Table~3) shows that removing multi-resolution processing reduces accuracy by only 1.76 percentage points (94.66\% $\rightarrow$ 92.90\%). Critically, XFuse \textit{without} multi-resolution processing (92.90\%) still outperforms the best baseline (CNN-Only: 89.13\%) by 3.77 percentage points, confirming that the performance gains are attributable to the cross-attention fusion and transformer components, not the resolution strategy.
   325→\end{itemize}
   326→
   327→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Added explicit fairness clarification paragraph in Section~4.2; added isolation sentence in the ablation discussion (Section~4.4).
   328→
   329→\textbf{R2-4: Data acquisition dates for reproducibility}
   330→\begin{quote}
   331→\textit{``The paper mentions Northern Finland during winter 2025 and citations include 2025 papers. [...] The authors should ensure the specific dates of acquisition are listed to ensure reproducibility.''}
   332→\end{quote}
   333→We thank the reviewer for this important reproducibility concern. We have added Table~2 (Section~4.1) with a full dataset summary, and we correct a labelling error in Sections~4.4 and~6 where the manuscript erroneously referred to ``winter 2025''; the dataset covers \textbf{December 2024 -- July 2025}, spanning the snow-free summer through the autumn snow-onset transition in northern Lapland. We apologise for this confusion.
   334→
   335→\textbf{Dataset provenance (three-stage pipeline).} The data collection proceeded in three stages, and we describe all three to give the reviewer the full picture:
   336→
   337→\begin{enumerate}
   338→  \item \textbf{Raw archive.} We downloaded a raw archive of \textbf{178 co-registered Sentinel-1/Sentinel-2 acquisition pairs} (407\,GB total) from the ESA Copernicus Data Space Ecosystem (\url{https://dataspace.copernicus.eu}), spanning \textbf{December 2024 to July 2025} across \textbf{45 MGRS tiles} in Finland (60--70.5\textdegree{}N, 20--32\textdegree{}E, UTM zones 33--36), with a Sentinel-2 maximum cloud coverage filter of 20\% applied at retrieval time.
   339→
   340→  \item \textbf{Quality-controlled preprocessing.} After radiometric calibration, terrain correction (SAR), atmospheric correction (optical), and geometric co-registration, \textbf{28 W-band tiles} (northern Finland, 64--72\textdegree{}N) with complete preprocessing pipelines were retained. The geographic focus on W-band tiles reflects the snow-monitoring objective and the availability of GFSC reference coverage for those tiles; V-band and other tiles were retained in the raw archive but not carried through the full preprocessing chain.
   341→
   342→  \item \textbf{Triplet matching.} Constructing training samples required assembling temporally coherent \textit{triplets} of co-located Sentinel-1, Sentinel-2, and GFSC acquisitions. This step is non-trivial: Sentinel-1 has a 6-day revisit cycle, Sentinel-2 a 5-day cycle, and GFSC products are 7-day gap-filled composites with their own composite dating windows. Differences in revisit periods, orbital geometries, and composite dating substantially reduce the number of valid co-temporal combinations. After applying a strict 30-day temporal alignment window, \textbf{29 matched (S1, S2, GFSC) triplets} from \textbf{19 unique W-band MGRS tiles} were obtained and used for training, validation, and testing.
   343→\end{enumerate}
   344→
   345→The 29 triplets span \textbf{Dec 2024--July 2025}, covering the transition from snow-free summer conditions through autumn snow onset in northern Lapland, and together represent the full 0--100\% fractional snow cover range in the dataset. GFSC composites are available from the Copernicus Land Monitoring Service HRWSI portal (\url{https://land.copernicus.eu/en/products/snow}). The revised Table~2 (Section~4.1) summarises each sensor's key parameters and archive scope to support direct archive retrieval.
   346→
   347→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Added Table~2 (sensor parameters and archive scope) and revised ``Data Acquisition Details'' paragraph in Section~4.1 with the three-stage provenance description; corrected ``northern Finland winter 2025'' $\rightarrow$ ``northern Finland (December 2024 -- July 2025)'' in Sections~4.4 and~6.
   348→
   349→\textbf{R2-5: Formatting inconsistency (10m$\to$60m$\to$10m)}
   350→\begin{quote}
   351→\textit{``Abstract: 10m$\to$60m$\to$10m formatting is slightly inconsistent in the text body.''}
   352→\end{quote}
   353→We thank the reviewer for catching this inconsistency. We have conducted a full formatting sweep across all manuscript sections to standardize the multi-resolution notation. Specifically:
   354→
   355→\begin{itemize}
   356→    \item The full three-stage notation now consistently uses $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ (with unit inside math mode) throughout the abstract, methodology, discussion, and conclusion sections.
   357→    \item Two-stage references in Section 3.2 sub-headings (``Resolution Aggregation'' and ``Guided Disaggregation'') were corrected from mixed formatting (plain text \texttt{m} outside math mode) to the consistent $\text{m}$ format matching the rest of the manuscript.
   358→    \item Comparison operators with resolution values (e.g., $\geq$60m, $<$60m) were also standardized across all sections.
   359→\end{itemize}
   360→
   361→\textit{Changes in manuscript:} Section 3.2 (lines 33, 45: corrected arrow notation formatting); Section 1.1 (line 10: standardized comparison operator format).
   362→
   363→\textbf{R2-6: Missing citation ``[? ]''}
   364→\begin{quote}
   365→\textit{``Page 6, line 5 of Section 2.1: `[? ]' indicates a missing citation.''}
   366→\end{quote}
   367→We thank the reviewer for identifying this error. The missing citation was caused by an incorrect bibliography key (\texttt{riggs2017overview}) that did not match any entry in our bibliography file. We have corrected this to reference \citet{riggs2017modis}, which properly cites the VIIRS snow cover product algorithm documentation.
   368→
   369→\textit{Change in manuscript:} Section 2.1, Line 8: \replaced{\textbackslash citep\{riggs2017modis\}}{\textbackslash citep\{riggs2017overview\}}
   370→
   371→% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   372→% CONCLUSION
   373→% 
   374→% Conclude the letter and express your gratitude to the editor and reviewers.
   375→% ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   376→\section*{Conclusion}
   377→We are confident that the revisions have significantly enhanced the quality, clarity, and rigor of our manuscript. The constructive feedback from the reviewers has allowed us to refine our work and present a more accurate and comprehensive study. We believe that the revised manuscript now meets the high standards expected by the ISPRS Journal of Photogrammetry and Remote Sensing, and we sincerely hope it will be considered favorably for publication.\\
   378→
   379→In the following attachment, we provide our responses to reviewer comments and suggestions in point-by-point track changes by color highlighting. The revision has been undertaken with the consent of all co-authors, and their contributions are duly acknowledged. Once again, we thank you and the reviewers for your invaluable input and the opportunity to improve our work. We look forward to your feedback on our revised submission.\\
   380→
   381→Sincerely,
   382→
   383→Getnet Demil on behalf of all co-authors
   384→
   385→Center for Machine Vision and Signal Analysis (CMVS), University of Oulu, Finland
   386→
   387→\clearpage
   388→% The reviewer_comments.tex file should contain the original comments from the reviewers.
   389→\input{reviewer_comments}
   390→
   391→\bibliographystyle{apalike}
   392→\bibliography{biblography}
   393→\end{document}
   394→

